<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Quitu-Cara</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1490 CE (approx.) · capital Quito</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The peoples of the Quito basin before the Incas, remembered in Ecuadorian tradition as the Kingdom of Quito ruled by the Shyris, a story many historians doubt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Quitu were the inhabitants of the highland basin where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> now stands, settled since at least the first millennium CE. In 1789 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-velasco">Juan de Velasco</a> wrote that the Cara people came from the coast around 980 and founded a kingdom ruled by kings called Shyris, which united the northern highlands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Archaeology shows a landscape of local chiefdoms around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and its valleys, with farming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a> and markets where goods from the coast and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> were traded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-velasco">Velasco</a>'s account of a centralised kingdom with a line of Shyri kings has no support from earlier chronicles and is widely regarded as legend, though it became a cornerstone of Ecuadorian national identity.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> region fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> in the late 15th century and became their northern capital under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atahualpa">Atahualpa's</a> capture, the Inca general Rumiñahui held Quito until the Spanish took it in 1534.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/quitu-cara.jpg" alt="Quitu-Cara" width="320" height="345"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/